unts the matched elements in the longest common subsequence, across 36 demonstrated cues. Exact sequence requires all three colors in order. A grounded command must match the action, object or color, and destination, allowing only the scorer’s fixed noun equivalences."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"research-table-wrap","role":"region","aria-label":"Matched T3 diagnostic · 30 clips","tabIndex":0,"children":["$","$L19",null,{"className":"research-table","children":[["$","caption",null,{"children":"Matched T3 diagnostic · 30 clips"}],["$","$L1a",null,{"children":["$","$L1b",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c","System",{"scope":"col","children":"System"}],["$","$L1c","Correct cup",{"scope":"col","children":"Correct cup"}],["$","$L1c","Grounded lift command",{"scope":"col","children":"Grounded lift command"}]]}]}],["$","$L1d",null,{"children":[["$","$L1b","0",{"className":"","children":[["$","$L1c","0",{"scope":"row","children":"No-memory VLM"}],["$","$L1e","1",{"children":"11/30 (36.7%)"}],["$","$L1e","2",{"children":"11/30 (36.7%)"}]]}],["$","$L1b","1",{"className":"","children":[["$","$L1c","0",{"scope":"row","children":"HiMe"}],["$","$L1e","1",{"children":"10/30 (33.3%)"}],["$","$L1e","2",{"children":"10/30 (33.3%)"}]]}],["$","$L1b","2",{"className":"ours-row","children":[["$","$L1c","0",{"scope":"row","children":"REMM"}],["$","$L1e","1",{"children":"30/30 (100.0%)"}],["$","$L1e","2",{"children":"30/30 (100.0%)"}]]}]]}]]}]}]]}]
2c:["$","div",null,{"className":"prose","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Remembering is not the same as grounding"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"HiMe and REMM recall the demonstrated sequence similarly well. Their difference appears when that memory must identify an object and produce the next instruction. Destination recognition alone remains strongest for the no-memory baseline."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"On T3, the no-memory baseline always selects the middle cup and matches the fixed-set majority reference of 11/30. Uniform random selection is 33.3% in expectation. REMM’s 30/30 has a Wilson 95% interval of 88.6–100%; it does not establish perfect tracking under real-world occlusion or appearance changes."}]]}]
2d:["$","details",null,{"className":"result-details","children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"Benchmark status and evaluation scope"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"prose","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The scored snapshot contains only these 42 clips. Additional intervention-revision and distractor-robust tracks are specified but are not part of the reported score. The legacy snapshot stores human approval verdicts without reviewer-count or adjudication records; a blinded second review remains required for a fully documented benchmark release."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Ground-truth answers stay scorer-only. The intended evaluation interface uses opaque clip IDs, causal frame access, open-vocabulary T2 grounding, and the same left/middle/right answer space for every T3 clip."}]]}]]}]
